Subject: extensions: the profile answers the externally-connectable question for a web page

Patch 0116 composed an `extensions` axis and left it with no caller on
purpose: a profile carried `extensions.externally_connectable`,
`base::apostate::Profile` parsed it, `Profile::extensions_externally_connectable()`
answered, and no page observed anything. This is the consumer. It is one
predicate, in the one function that decides the surface.

WHAT THE ONE PREDICATE IS

`CanWebpageContextConnectExternally`
(extensions/renderer/native_extension_bindings_system.cc) walks the
renderer's main-thread extension set and returns true on the first manifest
whose `externally_connectable.matches` covers the context URL.
`UpdateBindingsForContext` reads that answer twice and nothing else feeds
either read: once to set `set_accessor_on_browser`, which is why a matching
page also gets `window.browser`, and once to call `set_accessor("runtime")`.
So the whole surface hangs off one boolean, and one boolean reproduces it.

It reproduces it exactly rather than approximately, because the question is
asked of the whole set at once. The loop returns on the first match and never
reports which extension matched; no caller can ask "which one", and
`GetSerializationFormat` in runtime_hooks_delegate.cc deliberately returns
the same JSON format for "target not installed" as for "installed but not
connectable from this URL", with the comment that this is to stop untrusted
web pages discovering what is installed. A profile that says "some installed
extension names this page" is therefore indistinguishable from the set that
would have said it.

The scheme test is the manifest. `externally_connectable.matches` of
`["http://*/*", "https://*/*"]` is MetaMask's shape, and it is the shape
measured: on stock Chrome 153 with a MetaMask-shaped unpacked extension in a
fresh profile, `https://example.com/` gets `chrome.runtime` and
`window.browser` and `file:///…` and `about:blank` get neither. Switching the
same manifest to `<all_urls>` is what makes `file://` gain them. An
unconditional `return true` would reproduce the `<all_urls>` install, which is
the rarer one and the louder claim, so the predicate tests
`SchemeIsHTTPOrHTTPS()` on the context URL. `about:blank` falls out of the
same test: its script context URL is not an http or https URL.

WHY IT IS ADDITIVE AND NOT AUTHORITATIVE

`nullopt` falls through, which is `--fingerprint=host` and the only correct
answer there: the host's own extension set decides.

An explicit `false` also falls through rather than suppressing a real
extension's match, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. 0116's
row argued that absence is served as an explicit `false` so the host cannot
answer for the persona, and for the case it was written about -- an operator
running Apostate in a user-data-dir with no extensions, which is the default
-- an additive gate gives exactly that: the drawn state is the served state.
The case where the two differ is a user-data-dir that really does carry a
wallet extension, and there suppression is the worse answer, not the better
one. That page is already being shown the extension's content script, its
injected `window.ethereum`-shaped objects and its web-accessible resources;
withdrawing only the `runtime` accessor from underneath them composes a state
no install produces, which is incoherence added rather than removed. And the
browser process honours the manifest either way, so suppression would not
even be load-bearing: `message_service.cc` re-checks `externally_connectable`
against `util::GetURLForExtensionPermissionCheck(source_render_frame_host)`
before opening an external channel. An operator who wants the persona's
answer to be the whole answer installs nothing, which is what a composed
identity does by default.

WHAT THE BROWSER PROCESS STILL DECIDES, UNCHANGED

Read, and not touched by this patch:
`extensions/browser/api/messaging/message_service.cc` `OpenChannelToExtension`
accepts a `MessagingEndpoint::Type::kWebPage` channel from any frame and then,
for an external relationship, re-checks the target's `externally_connectable`
in the browser process against the source frame's URL; on failure it returns
`kReceivingEndDoesntExistError`, with the comment "Important: use
kReceivingEndDoesntExistError here so that we don't leak information about
this extension". That check is what keeps a real installed extension
receiving only what it declared, and widening the renderer gate does not
reach it: a page on a composed identity can now *call*
`chrome.runtime.sendMessage(someRealId, …)`, and the browser answers it
exactly as it answers a page with a non-matching wallet extension installed
-- same error, same serialization format, for an id that does not exist, one
that exists and declared this URL to another id, and one that exists and
declared nothing. There is no enumeration oracle to gain, which is also why
no renderer-side fabrication is needed for the failure path: the real one
already produces the measured string. `extension_frame_host.cc` forwards this
mojo call unconditionally and has no `bad_message::ReceivedBadMessage` on the
path, so a renderer that now asks more often cannot be killed for it.

Nothing else is edited, and nothing else needs to be. The feature file
already grants `runtime.connect`, `runtime.sendMessage` and
`runtime.lastError` to `web_page` over `<all_urls>` while keeping `getURL`,
`getManifest`, `onMessage` and the rest to extension contexts, which is
exactly why the measured web-page surface is `connect`, `sendMessage`, the
six enum objects and the `id`/`dynamicId` accessors and nothing more.
`runtime_hooks_delegate.cc` already installs `id` and `dynamicId` as native
data properties with an empty setter, so on a web page they exist, read
`undefined`, and swallow assignment. The whole observable comes out of the
real binding path.

NOT A COMPONENT EXTENSION

The alternative that needs no renderer edit is to ship an inert component
extension declaring `externally_connectable` for http and https, the way
`hangout_services` does today and `cryptotoken` did until Chrome 98. It is
rejected. A component extension has a fixed id, and a fixed extension id is
a cross-instance correlator that survives every other axis: two launches that
expose the same id are the same install whatever else was drawn. It would
also add a background service worker and a `chrome-extension://` origin with
resources, which is a great deal more new observable than one predicate.
The axis names no id and installs nothing.

VERIFIED

`scripts/checkfile.sh extensions/renderer/native_extension_bindings_system.cc`
compiles. This wave does not build, so what follows is the expectation the
driver at `/tmp/w2/ChromeRuntimeGate/probe.js` asserts, and the part of it
that is already measured.

The driver takes a present seed and an absent seed, launches the binary
twice with a fresh `--user-data-dir` and no extension installed, and visits
`https://example.com/` and a `file://` page in each. On this host (macOS
persona, 14 logical cores, 36 GiB, Metal) the `extensions` axis draws
`externally-connectable` for `--fingerprint=4` and `absent` for
`--fingerprint=1`; `--fingerprint-explain` prints the drawn option on the
`extensions` line once 0116 is in a build, and the reference resolver in
`scripts/profile_resolver.py` agrees with this binary on every other axis
for both seeds, which is how the two seeds were found. 11 of the seeds 1..59
draw present, which is the 75/25 table realised on a small sample.

Expected, `--fingerprint=4`, `https://example.com/`:

    Object.getOwnPropertyNames(chrome)     ["loadTimes","csi","app","runtime"]
    typeof window.browser                  "object"
    Object.getOwnPropertyNames(browser)    ["runtime"]
    browser.runtime === chrome.runtime      true
    Object.getOwnPropertyNames(runtime)    dynamicId, id, connect, sendMessage,
                                           ContextType, OnInstalledReason,
                                           OnRestartRequiredReason, PlatformArch,
                                           PlatformNaclArch, PlatformOs,
                                           RequestUpdateCheckStatus
    chrome.runtime.id                      undefined  ('id' in runtime true)
    chrome.runtime.dynamicId               undefined
    'lastError' in own props               false
    getURL / getManifest                   absent
    sendMessage(<32-char uninstalled id>, {})
        rejects Error: Could not establish connection. Receiving end does not
        exist.
    connect(<same id>)
        returns a Port, then onDisconnect with lastError.message the same string

Expected, `--fingerprint=4`, `file://`, and `--fingerprint=1` on both URLs:

    Object.getOwnPropertyNames(chrome)     ["loadTimes","csi","app"]
    typeof chrome.runtime                  "undefined"
    typeof window.browser                  "undefined"
    hasOwnProperty(window,'browser')       false

Measured now, against the shipped pre-patch binary: 12 of the driver's 26
assertions already hold, and they are exactly the 12 above -- both URLs on
the absent seed and the `file://` URL on the present seed. All 14 of the
present-and-https assertions fail, which is the surface this patch adds and
the reason the driver is worth trusting when it passes. Those 12 values are
also, field for field, what stock Google Chrome 153.0.8010.48 gives a fresh
profile on the same two URLs, so the absent state is not merely stable, it is
the reference.

RESIDUALS

The present state is a claim about the machine's owner, not about the
machine, and a page that branches on `chrome.runtime` takes its extension
path: a wallet-connect flow will offer the injected-provider button, and a
few SSO widgets will try the extension handshake first and fall back when the
message is not answered. That is the same thing those pages do for a real
MetaMask user who has the extension disabled, so it is a real state, but it
is a visibly different page for 25% of identities and is recorded as such in
docs/LIMITATIONS.md.

The service-worker path is untouched here, which matches the TODO already in
the function: a web page's service worker context is not a `kWebPage`
context, so it never reaches this predicate and never gained the accessor to
begin with, on stock Chrome or on a composed identity.

Ledger: extensions.webpage-runtime, emit.extensions.can-webpage-connect-externally.

